        definition:
          'A function differentiable once in a region is automatically differentiable infinitely often there, and equals its Taylor series in any disc within the region. Nothing remotely like this holds for real functions, where a function can be differentiable once and not twice. The rigidity goes further: an analytic function is determined throughout a connected region by its values on any small arc, so it has no local freedom at all. These properties are why complex analysis delivers results such as the integral formula that have no real counterpart, and they all trace back to the direction-independence of the derivative.',
        takeaway: 'Differentiable once means differentiable forever, and an analytic function has no local freedom at all.',
        visual: 'rigidity-contrast',
